  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "We can use our knowledge of fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2.\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model for $\\dfrac{3}{10}{:}$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129810/assets/7441921087fa0748dc0de6843c45c93fd9c492bade22fc8ae31192fd9c943016.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplying $\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{10}$ by $\\color{blue}2$ means that we need to repeat the fraction $\\color{blue}2$ times.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129810/assets/88ba96e731a6242aecea0a6b059933e128efdf6bbb52f37a0a73e57a004ab96c.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we count the number of shaded pieces. \n\nThere are ${\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 6$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} \\times 2 = \\dfrac{6}{10}.\n$$",
